<p>Hundreds of people gathered in London on Sunday for the annual Al-Quds Day event after the UK government barred a planned march but allowed a static demonstration instead. The Metropolitan Police said officers arrested 12 people and were also investigating anti-Israeli chants allegedly heard at the rally. Authorities kept pro-Palestinian demonstrators and counter-protesters on opposite sides of the River Thames near parliament, limiting direct contact as both groups waved flags and held political placards. Home Secretary Shabana Mahmood said she approved the ban to prevent serious public disorder after police said the march was being organised by a group they described as supportive of the Iranian government, against a backdrop of wider Middle East tensions. Even with sharp disagreement over the war in Gaza and regional escalation involving Iran, London authorities still made space for rival demonstrations under tight policing rather than letting the day slide into direct confrontation.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/CCTE_ewb-Zru4b_NT36vb2EZaJAMC8JtEHkkXT0VtWa-YMV4AiA9eZgkSTRZrYdb05tHHT_2AQlo4hupMtIofLoED2Dt5k4My-UDExF78kw7' alt='People attend a pro-Palestine rally in London during the annual Al-Quds Day event.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Rare ban: Dawn said this was the first time London had banned a protest march since 2012.</li><li>Counter rally: Counter-demonstrators waved US, Israeli, and Lion and Sun Iranian flags at the rival gathering.</li><li>Participant voice: Jean Apps, 81, from south London, said she attended not only because she saw what had happened to Palestinians as unjust, but also because she opposed attacks on Iran.</li><li>Enforcement detail: Dawn said police arrested one protester for refusing to remove a face covering during the annual protest.</li></ul><blockquote>I just feel so strongly that what has happened to the Palestinians is so unjust - Jean Apps</blockquote><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>Shabana Mahmood: The home secretary backed police claims that the planned march was being organised by a group described as &quot;supportive of the Iranian regime,&quot; and said that made it a public-order risk in the current Middle East climate. (<a href='https://www.scmp.com/news/world/europe/article/3346690/police-arrest-12-hundreds-join-banned-pro-palestinian-march-london'>SCMP</a>)</li><li>Metropolitan Police: Their focus was on containing the turnout on the Thames embankment under government restrictions rather than letting it move through central London. (<a href='https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2026/3/15/thousands-attend-al-quds-day-rally-in-london'>Al Jazeera</a>)</li><li>Counter-demonstrators: Signs reading &quot;Make Iran great again&quot; and &quot;Standing with my Jewish friends&quot; showed that the counter-rally mixed pro-Israel messages with anti-regime Iranian politics. (<a href='https://www.dawn.com/news/1982523/hundreds-turn-out-for-pro-palestine-rally-in-london'>Dawn</a>)</li></ul><h3>Sources:</h3><ul><li><a href='https://www.straitstimes.com/world/europe/hundreds-join-banned-uk-pro-palestinian-march'>Hundreds join banned pro-Palestinian march in London</a> - straitstimes.com</li><li><a href='https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2026/3/15/thousands-attend-al-quds-day-rally-in-london'>A dozen arrests as hundreds attend Al-Quds Day rally in London</a> - aljazeera.com</li><li><a href='https://www.scmp.com/news/world/europe/article/3346690/police-arrest-12-hundreds-join-banned-pro-palestinian-march-london'>Police arrest 12 as hundreds join banned pro-Palestinian march in London</a> - scmp.com</li><li><a href='https://www.dawn.com/news/1982523/hundreds-turn-out-for-pro-palestine-rally-in-london'>Hundreds turn out for pro-Palestine rally in London</a> - dawn.com</li></ul>